+        <h2 id="opa-w-title">OPA-W · Open Photonic Weight Memory</h2>
+        <p>OPA-W asks whether photonic cells can hold AI weights and use them for computation without repeatedly moving those weights to a separate processor. The research will compare streamed digital weights, optical readout with separate compute, and calculation with stationary optical weights.</p>
+        <p>The three browser simulations illustrate those ideas. All nine research phases are planned and unregistered for execution.</p>
+        <ul>
+          <li><a href="#photonic-circuit-lab/weight-memory">Chip 13 · Photonic Weight Memory</a> programs and probes a model cell.</li>
+          <li><a href="#photonic-circuit-lab/in-memory-computing">Chip 14 · Photonic In-Memory Computing</a> multiplies inputs by stationary weights.</li>
+          <li><a href="#photonic-circuit-lab/stored-weight-network">Chip 15 · Photonic Neural Network</a> combines three stored-weight layers with electronic activations.</li>
+        </ul>
+        <div class="ow-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="OPA-W research phases">
+          <table class="data-table">
+            <caption>Nine research phases</caption>
+            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Phase</th><th scope="col">Deliverable and gate</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
+            <tbody>
+              <tr><th scope="row">OPA-W-001 · Materials and architecture evidence</th><td>Compare primary evidence and baselines. Trace assumptions and select candidates against matched requirements.</td><td>Planned</td></tr>
+              <tr><th scope="row">OPA-W-002 · Multilevel cell</th><td>Compare ideal and calibrated models against binary storage. Set an error target before claiming 16 usable states and report drift, retention, write energy and uncertainty.</td><td>Planned</td></tr>
+              <tr><th scope="row">OPA-W-003 · Addressable array</th><td>Model a 16 × 16 array with routing and write isolation. Quantify addressing failures, crosstalk, yield, footprint and peripheral overhead.</td><td>Planned</td></tr>
+              <tr><th scope="row">OPA-W-004 · Parallel optical readout</th><td>Compare spatial, wavelength and time multiplexing. Account for useful bandwidth, latency, error, energy and detector or ADC limits.</td><td>Planned</td></tr>
+              <tr><th scope="row">OPA-W-005 · Photonic in-memory MVM</th><td>Compare signed matrix–vector multiplication against NumPy/PyTorch and existing OPA models with paired inputs, numerical error and matched system metrics.</td><td>Planned</td></tr>
+              <tr><th scope="row">OPA-W-006 · 3D and modular architecture</th><td>Compare planar, free-space and stacked modules with independent access, realistic loss, crosstalk, packaging and heat removal.</td><td>Planned</td></tr>
+              <tr><th scope="row">OPA-W-007 · Million-weight scaling</th><td>Validate tiled multiplication on a small workload and separate modeled scale from extrapolation. Account for routing, programming and utilization.</td><td>Planned</td></tr>
+              <tr><th scope="row">OPA-W-008 · Billion-weight feasibility</th><td>Assess dense and MoE scenarios at 7B, 70B and 405B with activation and KV-cache traffic. Bound resources, quality, energy and cost.</td><td>Planned</td></tr>
+              <tr><th scope="row">OPA-W-009 · Physical validation</th><td>Confirm process and lab access for a small test array. Measure transmission, retention, endurance, disturbance, precision and computation error.</td><td>Planned</td></tr>
+            </tbody>
+          </table>
